68
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:53,360
Fast forward five years.

69
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:56,520
Alex has made the payments and kept the
car running.

70
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:58,160
The loan is gone.

71
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,160
But before we total the cost, there's one
more number we need.

72
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:04,060
What the car is worth now.

73
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:08,060
Let's assume Alex can sell it for $15
,000.

74
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:11,880
That's a scenario assumption, not a resale
forecast.

75
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:14,500
Some cars hold value better.

76
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:19,080
Others lose more, especially with higher
mileage or poor condition.

77
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:24,660
The difference between the $30 ,000
purchase price and the $15

78
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:27,840
,000 sale price is $15 ,000.

79
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:29,560
That's depreciation.

80
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:33,160
The value Alex used up while owning the
car.

81
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,940
Here's where car cost math often goes
wrong.

82
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:41,960
You cannot add every loan payment and then
add depreciation on top.

83
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:45,280
The principal in the payments already pays
for the car.

84
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,200
Counting both would count that purchase
twice.

85
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:51,480
One clean method is to track cash.

86
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:56,800
Add the down payment, all 60 loan
payments, sales tax, insurance,

87
00:04:57,520 --> 00:04:59,040
fuel, maintenance and registration.

88
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:04,700
For Alex, that's about $64 ,528 paid out.

89
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:10,440
Now subtract the $15 ,000 Alex receives
from selling the car.

90
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:12,180
That money comes back.

91
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:16,920
The remaining cost is about $49 ,528

92
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,880
for five years of ownership.

93
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:24,560
Round it and this $30 ,000 car cost about
$49

94
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:27,560
,500 to own for five years.

95
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:32,680
That's about $825 a month, averaged across
the full

96
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:33,080
period.

97
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:38,440
Why is that lower than the roughly $995
monthly

98
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:43,000
cash budget? Because the ownership average
accounts for upfront costs

99
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,220
and the car's resale value.

100
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:49,520
Cash flow and total ownership costs answer
different questions.

101
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:51,960
You can also add depreciation,

102
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:54,780
interest and the operating costs directly.

103
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:57,320
$15 ,000 in lost value,

104
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,580
about $5 ,078 in interest,

105
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:04,440
then tax, insurance, fuel, maintenance and
registration.

106
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:06,140
Same answer.

107
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:07,600
No double counting.

108
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:13,020
An insurance quote that's $100 more per
month adds $6

109
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,740
,000 over five years.

110
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:20,060
A resale price $3 ,000 lower adds $3 ,000
to ownership

111
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:20,520
cost.

112
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:23,560
Small assumptions deserve a second look.

113
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:26,640
Does that mean Alex shouldn't own a car?

114
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:27,460
No.

115
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:32,540
A car can make work possible, save time
and connect you to people you care

116
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:36,940
about. Knowing the cost helps you decide
what that access is worth.

117
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:40,240
It also makes comparisons fairer.

118
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:42,500
A cheaper car may need more repairs.

119
00:06:43,460 --> 00:06:45,320
A newer car may lose more value.

120
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:49,160
An electric car changes fuel and charging
costs.

121
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,220
The winner depends on the full set of
assumptions.

122
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:58,180
For example, a $20 ,000 car with the same
$3 ,000 down,

123
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:02,800
7 % rate and 60 month term has a payment
around

124
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,860
$337.

125
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:08,180
That's roughly $198 less each month.

126
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:11,380
That lower payment is useful breathing
room.

127
00:07:11,940 --> 00:07:13,660
It isn't a complete comparison yet.

128
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:17,360
Alex still needs estimates for insurance,
repairs,

129
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:19,620
fuel and resale value.

130
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:23,200
Run the same five year calculation for
both cars.

131
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:28,520
Before signing, get the out the door
price, the full loan terms and an

132
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:33,000
insurance quote. Estimate your actual
mileage, budget for maintenance.

133
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:37,880
Then choose a reasonable ownership period
and a conservative resale

134
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:38,280
assumption.

135
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:44,280
Make two budgets. First, can I handle the
cash payments including the

136
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:46,680
upfront money and occasional large bills?

137
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:51,960
Second, what will owning this car cost
over the years I expect to keep it?

138
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:56,300
Keeping a paid off car can reduce your
budget pressure too.

139
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:59,680
But keep allowing for maintenance and
value loss.

140
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:02,760
A zero dollar loan payment feels great.

141
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:06,020
It doesn't turn the rest of the dashboard
into zeros.

142
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:08,820
Alex still likes the blue car.

143
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:12,200
Now though, the decision comes with open
eyes.

144
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:15,620
Instead of asking only, can I afford this
payment?

145
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:19,300
Alex asks, what will this car really cost
me to own?

146
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:23,060
Before your next test drive, build the
whole budget.

147
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:26,600
Because the number on the windshield tells
you what you're buying.

148
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:29,800
The full cost tells you what you're
committing to.
